# Typewriter Text Must Use Fixed Vertical Layout

When using a typewriter (character-by-character reveal) effect, the layout **must not shift vertically** as new characters or lines appear. Use a `ZStack` with the full text rendered invisibly (`.hidden()`) to reserve the exact space needed upfront.

```swift
// ❌ BAD - Layout shifts as text grows, pushing content below it
Text(displayedText)
    .font(.system(size: 17, design: .serif))
    .lineSpacing(10)
    .frame(minHeight: 60)

// ✅ GOOD - Full text reserves space; visible text fills in without shifting
ZStack(alignment: .center) {
    // Invisible full text reserves the exact vertical space
    Text(fullText)
        .font(.system(size: 17, design: .serif))
        .lineSpacing(10)
        .padding(.horizontal, 36)
        .hidden()

    // Visible typewriter text
    Text(displayedText)
        .font(.system(size: 17, design: .serif))
        .lineSpacing(10)
        .padding(.horizontal, 36)
}
```

**Key rules:**
- The hidden `Text` and visible `Text` must use **identical** font, lineSpacing, and padding so their measurements match exactly.
- Never rely on `minHeight` or hardcoded frame heights for typewriter text; they break across locales and font sizes.
- This applies to both custom typewriter implementations and the shared `TypewriterText` component.

