# Never Use Bare Array Subscript on Dynamic Indices

**CRITICAL**: Never access an array element with `array[index]` when the index comes from stored state, user data, or computed values. Always bounds-check first or use safe alternatives.

This caused a production crash: `completeDay()` advanced `currentDay` past the end of `plan.days`, then `getTodayReading()` did `plan.days[currentDay]` without checking bounds.

```swift
// ❌ BAD - Crashes if index is out of range
let day = plan.days[progress.currentDay]

// ✅ GOOD - Guard bounds before subscripting
guard progress.currentDay >= 0, progress.currentDay < plan.days.count else {
    return plan.days.last
}
let day = plan.days[progress.currentDay]

// ✅ GOOD - Use .first / .last for edge access
return plan.days.first

// ✅ GOOD - Use safe subscript or indices.contains
guard plan.days.indices.contains(index) else { return nil }

// ✅ GOOD - Clamp when writing the index
progress.currentDay = min(newValue, plan.days.count - 1)
```

**Rules:**
- When writing an index to storage (UserDefaults, state), clamp it within valid range.
- When reading from storage to subscript an array, always guard `index < array.count`.
- In `#Preview` blocks, use `.first` instead of `[0]`.
- `ForEach(array)` and `.prefix()` are safe; raw subscript with a variable index is not.

