# Bible JSON Verse Content May Contain Mixed Types (Not Just Strings)

The `por_blj` (Portuguese) Bible data from `bible.helloao.org` includes footnote reference objects inline in verse content arrays alongside plain strings:

```json
"content": ["Ai da suja", {"noteId": 4}, "e contaminada"]
```

**Never decode `APIVerseContent.content` as `[String]`** — this crashes for any chapter containing footnotes.

```swift
// ❌ BAD - Fails to decode chapters with footnotes (e.g. Zephaniah 3, many others)
struct APIVerseContent: Codable {
    let content: [String]
}

// ✅ GOOD - Use a mixed-type enum that skips non-string elements
private enum VerseContentElement: Codable {
    case text(String)
    case footnote

    init(from decoder: Decoder) throws {
        let container = try decoder.singleValueContainer()
        if let str = try? container.decode(String.self) { self = .text(str) }
        else { self = .footnote }
    }
    var textValue: String? { if case .text(let s) = self { return s } else { return nil } }
}

struct APIVerseContent: Codable {
    let content: [VerseContentElement]
}

// Then join only the text parts:
let verseText = verseContent.content.compactMap(\.textValue).joined(separator: " ")
```

This applies to all translations using the API format (`spa_r09`, `por_blj`). Spanish currently has no footnotes but the same decoder is shared.

